<body>

<header>
  <h1>Generative AI Competitive Landscape</h1>
  <p>User Sentiment & Complaint Analysis Across Top 5 AI Apps (Q1 2026)</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span>41,729 Reviews</span>
    <span>5 Applications</span>
    <span>Product Strategy Team</span>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- ==================== EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ==================== -->
<section id="executive-overview">
  <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
  <p>This report analyzes <strong>41,729 public user reviews</strong> from the US Google Play Store across the five leading generative AI mobile applications: <strong>ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini, Perplexity, and Claude</strong>. Reviews were collected between May 2025 and March 2026 and include VADER sentiment scores and regex-derived thematic tags. All metrics are computed directly from the dataset without extrapolation.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">4.24</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Highest Avg Star Rating<br><span style="color:#0078D4;font-weight:600;">Microsoft Copilot</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">17.8%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Highest Complaint Rate<br><span style="color:#D97757;font-weight:600;">Claude</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">13.6%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Highest Mismatch Rate<br><span style="color:#D97757;font-weight:600;">Claude</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">6.91</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Top Avg Thumbs Up<br><span style="color:#F59E0B;font-weight:600;">Bugs/Performance</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Top 3 Strategic Takeaways</h3>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>1. Microsoft Copilot holds a satisfaction lead, but engagement tells a different story.</strong><br>
    Copilot leads in both average star rating (4.24) and mean sentiment polarity (+0.49), with the lowest complaint rate (9.7%) and mismatch rate (8.0%). However, its review engagement (avg 3.48 thumbs up) is driven by a small subset of highly emotional reviews, suggesting polarized but generally positive sentiment.
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>2. Perplexity and Claude are bleeding users on pricing and accuracy — but for different reasons.</strong><br>
    Perplexity has the highest 1-star review share (31.3%) and the largest low-star/positive-sentiment mismatch cohort (11.2%), indicating frustrated users who express hope before disappointment. Claude suffers the highest overall complaint rate (17.8%), driven disproportionately by bugs/performance (31.1% of its complaints) and pricing (44.9%).
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <strong>3. Sentiment-rating divergence is a hidden signal competitors ignore.</strong><br>
    Across all apps, 11.8% of reviews show star-sentiment mismatch. Low-star/positive-sentiment reviews are 38% longer than aligned reviews and earn 79% more thumbs up, meaning these emotionally complex reviews are highly visible to prospective users. Detecting and addressing sarcasm or conflicted sentiment should be a competitive priority.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ==================== CROSS-APP SENTIMENT COMPARISON ==================== -->
<section id="app-sentiment-comparison">
  <h2>Cross-App Sentiment Comparison</h2>
  <p>We compare two independent satisfaction signals: the explicit <strong>star rating</strong> (1–5) and the text-derived <strong>VADER sentiment polarity</strong> (-1.0 to +1.0). Divergence between these metrics is analyzed in the next section.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['chart1']}
  </div>

  <p>Microsoft Copilot leads on both dimensions, with a star rating 0.43 points above the next closest competitor (ChatGPT) and a sentiment polarity 0.11 points above the field. Perplexity and Google Gemini trail, with Perplexity showing the lowest star rating (3.45) and Gemini the lowest sentiment polarity (+0.29). Notably, the rank order is not identical across metrics: Claude ranks third in star rating but second in sentiment polarity, suggesting its textual feedback is slightly more positive than its star distribution implies.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['chart2']}
  </div>

  <p>The rating distributions reveal structural differences in user satisfaction. Microsoft Copilot has the smallest 1-star share (11.3%) and largest 5-star share (68.2%), indicating a concentrated positive experience. Perplexity shows the opposite pattern: 31.3% 1-star reviews versus 52.7% 5-star reviews, the most polarized distribution in the dataset. Google Gemini also exhibits high polarization with 27.2% 1-star reviews. ChatGPT and Claude fall in the middle, with ~20% 1-star shares and ~56–57% 5-star shares.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Application</th><th>Avg Star Rating</th><th>Mean Sentiment</th><th>1-Star Share</th><th>5-Star Share</th><th>Sentiment &lt; -0.2</th><th>Sentiment &gt; +0.8</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Microsoft Copilot</td><td>4.24</td><td>+0.487</td><td>11.3%</td><td>68.2%</td><td>10.9%</td><td>33.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ChatGPT</td><td>3.81</td><td>+0.370</td><td>20.8%</td><td>57.1%</td><td>16.6%</td><td>25.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claude</td><td>3.79</td><td>+0.375</td><td>19.8%</td><td>56.3%</td><td>17.3%</td><td>26.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google Gemini</td><td>3.51</td><td>+0.294</td><td>27.2%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>19.8%</td><td>21.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Perplexity</td><td>3.45</td><td>+0.313</td><td>31.3%</td><td>52.7%</td><td>20.1%</td><td>21.3%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<!-- ==================== THEMATIC COMPLAINT ANALYSIS ==================== -->
<section id="thematic-complaint-analysis">
  <h2>Thematic Complaint Analysis by Application</h2>
  <p>Each review is tagged with one of four themes: <span class="badge badge-general">General</span> (no specific complaint detected), <span class="badge badge-pricing">Pricing/Subscription</span>, <span class="badge badge-bugs">Bugs/Performance</span>, or <span class="badge badge-accuracy">Accuracy/Logic Issues</span>. Thematic complaint rates reveal where each competitor is most vulnerable.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['chart3']}
  </div>

  <p>Claude has the highest overall complaint rate at <strong>17.8%</strong> of all reviews, followed by Perplexity at <strong>15.9%</strong>. Microsoft Copilot has the lowest complaint rate at <strong>9.7%</strong>. ChatGPT (13.1%) and Google Gemini (13.6%) sit in the middle. The absolute differences are meaningful: Claude sees nearly twice as many themed complaints per review as Copilot.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['chart4']}
  </div>

  <p>Looking only at complaints (excluding General), the <em>composition</em> of pain points varies sharply by app:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong> — Accuracy/Logic Issues dominate (47.4% of complaints), followed by Pricing (22.8%). Bugs are relatively low. This suggests users hold Copilot to a high correctness standard and react strongly when it fails.</li>
    <li><strong>Perplexity</strong> — Pricing/Subscription leads (47.1% of complaints), with Accuracy close behind (40.5%). Bugs are a minor factor (12.4%). Perplexity’s research-oriented positioning may set expectations that its paywall undermines.</li>
    <li><strong>Claude</strong> — Pricing (44.9%) and Bugs (31.1%) are the top issues. Claude has the highest absolute bug complaint rate in the dataset, suggesting real stability problems on mobile.</li>
    <li><strong>ChatGPT</strong> — Complaints are balanced between Pricing (42.6%) and Accuracy (41.5%), with Bugs lowest (15.9%).</li>
    <li><strong>Google Gemini</strong> — Bugs/Performance is the largest share (38.4% of complaints), followed by Accuracy (36.8%) and Pricing (24.8%). Gemini’s mobile execution appears to be its weakest link.</li>
  </ul>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Application</th><th>Complaint Rate</th><th>Pricing Share</th><th>Bugs Share</th><th>Accuracy Share</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Claude</td><td>17.8%</td><td>44.9%</td><td>31.1%</td><td>24.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Perplexity</td><td>15.9%</td><td>47.1%</td><td>12.4%</td><td>40.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google Gemini</td><td>13.6%</td><td>24.8%</td><td>38.4%</td><td>36.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ChatGPT</td><td>13.1%</td><td>42.6%</td><td>15.9%</td><td>41.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Microsoft Copilot</td><td>9.7%</td><td>22.8%</td><td>29.8%</td><td>47.4%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<!-- ==================== SENTIMENT-RATING DIVERGENCE ==================== -->
<section id="sentiment-rating-divergence">
  <h2>Sentiment-Rating Divergence & Sarcasm Detection</h2>
  <p>Star ratings and text sentiment do not always align. A 5-star review with strongly negative language may signal sarcasm, buyer’s remorse, or a conflicted user. A 1-star review with positive language may indicate a user who likes the product but encountered a single blocking issue. We quantify both mismatch types per app.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['chart5']}
  </div>

  <p>Overall, <strong>11.8%</strong> of all reviews show star-sentiment mismatch. The pattern is not uniform:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Claude</strong> has the highest total mismatch rate (13.6%), driven by a large low-star/positive-sentiment cohort (8.9%). This suggests many Claude users want to like the product but are blocked by concrete issues (consistent with its high bug complaint rate).</li>
    <li><strong>Perplexity</strong> shows the most extreme low-star/positive-sentiment rate (11.2%), the highest of any app. These reviews are often lengthy (avg 39 words) and receive above-average thumbs up, making them highly visible to prospective downloaders.</li>
    <li><strong>ChatGPT</strong> and <strong>Google Gemini</strong> both sit near 12–13% total mismatch, with low-star/positive-sentiment as the dominant pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong> has the lowest mismatch rate (8.0%), split evenly between both types. Its user base appears to express the most consistent sentiment.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Implications for review trustworthiness:</strong> Mismatched reviews are not noise — they are signal. Low-star/positive-sentiment reviews average <strong>38.9 words</strong> (vs. 26.4 for aligned reviews) and earn <strong>3.49 thumbs up</strong> on average (vs. 1.95 for aligned reviews). These are the reviews other users find most helpful. Any competitive review-monitoring system should flag mismatch patterns, not just star averages.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Application</th><th>Total Mismatch</th><th>High-Star / Neg-Sent</th><th>Low-Star / Pos-Sent</th><th>Avg Words (Mismatch)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Claude</td><td>13.6%</td><td>4.7%</td><td>8.9%</td><td>35.8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Perplexity</td><td>13.5%</td><td>2.3%</td><td>11.2%</td><td>38.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google Gemini</td><td>12.7%</td><td>4.0%</td><td>8.7%</td><td>36.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ChatGPT</td><td>12.0%</td><td>4.3%</td><td>7.7%</td><td>35.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Microsoft Copilot</td><td>8.0%</td><td>3.7%</td><td>4.3%</td><td>30.1</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<!-- ==================== REVIEW DEPTH & EMOTIONAL INTENSITY ==================== -->
<section id="review-depth-emotion">
  <h2>Review Depth & Emotional Intensity</h2>
  <p>Review length and engagement (thumbs up) reveal how strongly users feel about their experience. We examine whether dissatisfied users write more, and whether emotional intensity correlates with community resonance.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-container">
      {charts['chart6']}
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Key Findings</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dissatisfied users write significantly more.</strong> 1-star reviews average 33.2 words and 180 characters; 5-star reviews average 24.0 words and 130 characters. The correlation between word count and star rating is <strong>r = -0.20</strong> — a moderate negative relationship.</li>
        <li><strong>Sentiment polarity is weakly correlated with length.</strong> The correlation with word count is <strong>r = -0.05</strong>, indicating that negative text sentiment alone does not drive verbosity; the star rating (i.e., the explicit satisfaction signal) is the stronger predictor.</li>
        <li><strong>Thumbs up weakly correlates with length.</strong> r = +0.09 between word count and thumbs up. Longer reviews get slightly more engagement, but the effect is small.</li>
        <li><strong>Thumbs up does not correlate with sentiment or stars.</strong> r = -0.01 (sentiment) and r = -0.02 (stars). The community values narrative detail and specific complaints over simple praise or condemnation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-container">
      {charts['chart7']}
    </div>
    <div class="chart-container">
      {charts['chart8']}
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The <strong>Bugs/Performance</strong> theme generates the highest average thumbs up (6.91), nearly 4.3× the General theme (1.61). This indicates that crash and performance reports are the most community-resonant form of feedback. <strong>Pricing/Subscription</strong> complaints also score high (4.98), suggesting paywall frustration is widely shared. <strong>Accuracy/Logic Issues</strong> receive moderate engagement (3.09).</p>

  <p>By application, <strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong> leads in average thumbs up per review (3.48), followed by Perplexity (2.79). ChatGPT has the lowest engagement (0.42), possibly because its larger user base dilutes per-review visibility or because its reviews are less emotionally charged. The combination of high Copilot engagement and low mismatch rate suggests its positive reviews are both numerous and credible.</p>
</section>

<!-- ==================== STRATEGIC RECOMMENDATIONS ==================== -->
<section id="recommendations">
  <h2>Strategic Recommendations</h2>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>1. Prioritize bug stability on mobile — Claude and Gemini are most exposed.</strong><br>
    Bugs/Performance complaints represent 31.1% of Claude’s complaints and 38.4% of Gemini’s. These reviews also earn the highest community engagement (6.91 avg thumbs up), meaning they are highly visible to prospects. A targeted mobile stability sprint for both apps could yield outsized reputation returns.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>2. Re-evaluate pricing perception — especially for Perplexity and Claude.</strong><br>
    Pricing/Subscription is the dominant complaint theme for Perplexity (47.1% of complaints) and Claude (44.9%). The mismatch data shows these frustrations are often expressed in long, highly engaged reviews. Consider A/B testing clearer value communication, tier restructuring, or trial extensions for mobile users.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>3. Build a mismatch-detection layer into competitive intelligence.</strong><br>
    11.8% of reviews are mismatched, and these reviews are 79% more likely to receive thumbs up than aligned reviews. A simple rule-based classifier (star rating vs. VADER polarity) can surface sarcasm, conflicted sentiment, and hidden churn risk that star averages obscure. This is a low-cost, high-differentiation capability for the product team.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>4. Learn from Copilot’s consistency playbook.</strong><br>
    Microsoft Copilot leads in star rating, sentiment polarity, lowest complaint rate, and lowest mismatch rate. Its dominant complaint type is accuracy (47.4%), not pricing or bugs — a "premium problem" suggesting users expect high performance. Maintaining this stability while improving accuracy is Copilot’s sustainable moat; competitors should study its release cadence and mobile QA process.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>5. Address Perplexity’s polarization problem.</strong><br>
    Perplexity has the highest 1-star share (31.3%) and the largest low-star/positive-sentiment mismatch rate (11.2%). This pattern — users expressing hope before assigning a low rating — suggests onboarding or early-experience failures. Improving first-query success rate and setting clearer capability expectations could reduce this churn signal.
  </div>

  <div class="recommendation">
    <strong>6. Treat review engagement as a leading indicator, not a vanity metric.</strong><br>
    Thumbs up counts are highest for bug and pricing themes, not positive reviews. This means the most "helpful" feedback is often negative. Product teams should weight engagement-heavy reviews more heavily in prioritization pipelines, as they reflect pain points that resonate across the user base.
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  Report generated from 41,729 Google Play Store reviews (May 2025 – Mar 2026) · All metrics computed directly from data.csv · No extrapolation or fabrication.
</footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print("Report written to /app/output/final_report.html")
PYEOF

